# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Proverbs'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'poetry'
SUBTITLE = 'The Fear of the LORD, Wisdom for Daily Life, and the Counsel of a Father'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Proverbs opens with “The proverbs of Solomon the son of David, king of Israel” (Prov. 1:1), gathers counsel on wisdom, the heart, speech, money, friendship and family, and adds proverbs “which the men of Hezekiah king of Judah copied out” (Prov. 25:1). It closes with a portrait of the virtuous woman (Prov. 31:10, 30).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'The book is introduced as the proverbs of Solomon, king of Israel, and of the men of Hezekiah king of Judah (Prov. 1:1; 25:1). Its sayings are rooted in the fear of the LORD and are placed in the Universal Truths Table except where a verse names the Israelite setting.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Proverbs', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — A Father’s Call to Wisdom (Chs. 1–9)'), ('p', 'The purpose of the book is stated and wisdom is set before the young: “The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge” (Prov. 1:7; 3:5–6; 4:23; 9:10).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Proverbs of Solomon (Chs. 10–22:16)'), ('p', 'Short sayings on the wise son, the soft answer, honest weights, the way that seems right and the training of a child (Prov. 10:1; 11:1; 14:12; 15:1; 22:6).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Sayings of the Wise (Chs. 22:17–24)'), ('p', 'Counsel on friends, the fear of man and the pursuit of wisdom (Prov. 13:20; 17:17; 24:3).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Hezekiah’s Collection (Chs. 25–29)'), ('p', 'Further proverbs of Solomon copied out by the men of Hezekiah king of Judah (Prov. 25:1; 27:17; 28:13; 29:25).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — Agur, Lemuel, and the Virtuous Woman (Chs. 30–31)'), ('p', 'Agur’s words, a king’s mother’s teaching, and the portrait of the woman who feareth the LORD (Prov. 30:5; 31:10, 30).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Proverbs', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Fear of the LORD</b> — “The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge: but fools despise wisdom and instruction” (Prov. 1:7).', '<b>Trust and Direction</b> — “Trust in the LORD with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding” (Prov. 3:5).', '<b>Guarding the Heart</b> — “Keep thy heart with all diligence; for out of it are the issues of life” (Prov. 4:23).', '<b>The Way That Seems Right</b> — “There is a way which seemeth right unto a man, but the end thereof are the ways of death” (Prov. 14:12).', '<b>The Soft Answer</b> — “A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger” (Prov. 15:1).', '<b>The LORD Directs</b> — “A man’s heart deviseth his way: but the LORD directeth his steps” (Prov. 16:9).', '<b>The Word of God Pure</b> — “Every word of God is pure: he is a shield unto them that put their trust in him” (Prov. 30:5).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Proverbs', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Proverbs points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>Wisdom Personified</b> — Wisdom speaks of being with the LORD “before his works of old” (Prov. 8:22), reported as written.', '<b>A Word That Shields</b> — “Every word of God is pure” (Prov. 30:5).', '<b>The Greater Than Solomon</b> — Solomon is the king named at the head of the book (Prov. 1:1); the Lord speaks of one greater than Solomon (Matt. 12:42).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Proverbs', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Proverbs speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Proverbs operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Proverbs in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Proverbs reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', []), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Proverbs that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Proverbs contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Proverbs of Solomon — “The proverbs of Solomon the son of David, king of Israel.” (Prov. 1:1).', 'A Wise Son — “The proverbs of Solomon. A wise son maketh a glad father.” (Prov. 10:1).', 'Hezekiah’s Men — “proverbs of Solomon, which the men of Hezekiah king of Judah copied out.” (Prov. 25:1).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Proverbs Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Proverbs and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Proverbs teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Proverbs', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Proverbs of Solomon', '“The proverbs of Solomon the son of David, king of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Prov. 1:1'], ['A Wise Son', '“The proverbs of Solomon. A wise son maketh a glad father.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Prov. 10:1'], ['Hezekiah’s Men', '“proverbs of Solomon, which the men of Hezekiah king of Judah copied out.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Prov. 25:1']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Proverbs that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Proverbs teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['The Fear of the LORD', '“The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge.”', 'Prov. 1:7'], ['Trust the LORD', '“Trust in the LORD with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding.”', 'Prov. 3:5'], ['He Directs the Paths', '“In all thy ways acknowledge him, and he shall direct thy paths.”', 'Prov. 3:6'], ['Keep the Heart', '“Keep thy heart with all diligence; for out of it are the issues of life.”', 'Prov. 4:23'], ['Honest Weights', '“A false balance is abomination to the LORD: but a just weight is his delight.”', 'Prov. 11:1'], ['The Way That Seems Right', '“There is a way which seemeth right unto a man.”', 'Prov. 14:12'], ['A Soft Answer', '“A soft answer turneth away wrath.”', 'Prov. 15:1'], ['The LORD Directs', '“A man’s heart deviseth his way: but the LORD directeth his steps.”', 'Prov. 16:9'], ['A Strong Tower', '“The name of the LORD is a strong tower: the righteous runneth into it, and is safe.”', 'Prov. 18:10'], ['The Counsel of the LORD', '“the counsel of the LORD, that shall stand.”', 'Prov. 19:21'], ['Confession', '“whoso confesseth and forsaketh them shall have mercy.”', 'Prov. 28:13'], ['Every Word Pure', '“Every word of God is pure: he is a shield unto them that put their trust in him.”', 'Prov. 30:5']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Proverbs without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Proverbs in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Begin with the fear of the LORD</b> — The beginning of knowledge (Prov. 1:7).', '<b>Trust Him with the whole heart</b> — Lean not on your own understanding (Prov. 3:5).', '<b>Guard the heart</b> — Out of it are the issues of life (Prov. 4:23).', '<b>Deal honestly</b> — A just weight is His delight (Prov. 11:1).', '<b>Answer softly</b> — A soft answer turneth away wrath (Prov. 15:1).', '<b>Let the LORD direct your steps</b> — The heart devises, the LORD directs (Prov. 16:9).', '<b>Run to His name</b> — A strong tower (Prov. 18:10).', '<b>Confess and forsake</b> — Mercy follows (Prov. 28:13).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Proverbs', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Proverbs in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['A Father’s Call to Wisdom (Chs. 1–9) — The fear of the LORD. <i>(Prov. 1:1, 7; 3:5–6; 4:23)</i>', 'The Proverbs of Solomon (Chs. 10–22) — Sayings on daily life. <i>(Prov. 10:1; 11:1; 14:12; 15:1; 16:9)</i>', 'Sayings of the Wise (Chs. 22–24) — Friends and the heart. <i>(Prov. 19:21)</i>', 'Hezekiah’s Collection (Chs. 25–29) — More of Solomon’s sayings. <i>(Prov. 25:1; 28:13)</i>', 'Agur and Lemuel (Chs. 30–31) — The pure word and the virtuous woman. <i>(Prov. 30:5; 31:10, 30)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Proverbs, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward wisdom, humility, and the fear of the LORD without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Proverbs simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Proverbs stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
